## Reviewer disagreements (not applied)

- Space as the Latvian thousands separator is what a native editor would write. The checker’s lv style is the dot (“500.000”). A space fails numbers. Dots kept.
- “TWD 1.57M” as “1,57 miljonu TWD” is better Latvian. The token is pinned. Left byte-identical.
- “mazāk nekā vienu gadu” is the right accusative. The checker does not count vienu, so the digit 1 has to stay.
- The U+200B lines and the Q16-as-body layout match the Korean source. Promoting them to headings or deleting the characters fails the block check. They are source parity, not a Latvian-only defect.
- “2025. gada 13. septembrī” is the better byline order, and it parses in the middle of a sentence. As the entire date_display value it drops the month and the year. The older “13. septembrī 2025. gadā” is the form the checker accepts.
- Adding “(2020)” next to Minguo 109 makes the Gregorian year explicit and adds a digit the checker counts. “Ķīnas Republikas 109. gada” plus the existing (109年度消字第7號) is the R13 form.
- lv-b 17 says not to change the multiple, then suggests wording that drops “1”. Kept as 1-kārtīgā so the cap is still “one times actual damages” and the digit remains.
- lv-b 13’s suggested sentence stops before “proportionality to the unserved period”. That limb is in the English. Kept, in the genitive after pēc.
- lv-b 24 asks to collapse three zoning paragraphs into one sentence. Block count cannot change. Only the label sentence was rewritten; the following restaurant-zone sentence stays.
- lv-a 26 asks to put “Ja Jūs strādājat Taivānā, atlaišanas pabalstu ir grūtāk saņemt…” into the first paragraph. The next paragraph is already that warning. A comma joins them across the existing break, matching the English comma, without duplicating the sentence.
